From: theycallmeboxy <82409831+theycallmeboxy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:58:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] pan/kmod: cap GPU memory at what a game can actually get

The kernel's OOM handling can't get GPU memory back in time: GPU pages
can't be swapped, and kbase only returns a process's GPU pages when its
GPU context is torn down at exit. When a process allocates GPU memory
until the system runs out, killing it frees nothing quickly, and the OOM
killer works through the rest of the system until the kernel panics. A
game reached ~9 GB of GPU memory on an 8 GB device this way; files
written shortly before the panic were lost.

Guard against it so that an out-of-memory situation ends in this process
rather than in the system:

 - cap this process's GPU buffers at what a game can actually get: RAM
   minus RAM/8 (clamped to [768 MiB, 2 GiB]) and 2.5 GiB for the OS and
   the game's CPU side, at least 4 GiB or half the RAM if less, at most
   75% of the RAM (8 GB: 4.2 GiB, 12 GB: 7.5 GiB, 16 GB: 10.9 GiB).
   PAN_MEM_LIMIT_MB overrides it, 0 disables the guard;
 - also refuse allocations while MemAvailable is below twice the
   critical level and swap can't make room (less than 1/8 of it free, or
   none). A game's CPU side normally gets swapped out when memory is
   low, so low MemAvailable alone is no reason to refuse;
 - if MemAvailable drops below the critical level (RAM/24 clamped to
   [256, 768] MiB) while this process holds more than that in GPU
   buffers, log what it holds and kill the process, which frees its GPU
   memory on exit.

Refused allocations fail with VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY and log the
request, the process's holdings by size class, MemAvailable and
SwapFree. /proc/meminfo is read once per 64 MiB of new allocations, and
on every allocation while memory is low. Imported buffers aren't counted.

The GPU shares RAM with everything else, and engines size their pools
from the heap size as if it were dedicated VRAM, so unless
heap_memory_percent is set PanVK reports the same limit as its heap size
(instead of 75% of RAM), and VK_EXT_memory_budget reports what the
process may still allocate under it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QAYrqSPd4TgpVaKLqz5cYR

Purpose: stop a runaway GPU allocator from exhausting system memory (GPU pages can't be swapped and kbase frees them only at context teardown, so the kernel OOM killer cannot recover them and the kernel panics): cap each process's GPU memory at what a game can actually get (RAM-scaled, also reported as the heap size), refuse when memory is low and swap is full, and have a process still holding GPU memory at a critical MemAvailable log and kill itself
Upstream-commit: none (generic pan_kmod + PanVK heap size; upstream candidate)
Tested GPU: Mali-G615 MC6 (Anbernic RG477M, Dimensity 8300, v11, 8 GB)
Tested Kbase UAPI: CSF 1.21 / MediaTek
Dependencies: csf-v11/085
Validation test: 8 GB: limit/heap 4284 MiB, critical 325 MiB. memhog: refused at 4234 MiB held with 2 GB free (retries refused); with PAN_MEM_LIMIT_MB=7000 grows to 6154 MiB while swap absorbs pressure, then logs and SIGKILLs itself at MemAvailable 320 MiB, no lmkd kills. Games (GameNative, PanVK): Valheim 2.05 GB GPU, MemAvailable min 623 MiB, no refusals, 57 fps; Megabonk 60 fps; Jupiter Hell (previously panicked the kernel at ~9 GB) has its 3.6 GB request refused and reaches its loading screen; Descenders refused at 4.08 GB and exits (stock driver reached 5.3 GB with 295 MiB free); racetest all cases 0/100 stale, controls 100/100; drawbench image checksums identical to csf-v11/193 (8 tests)
Channel: compatibility, unsafe
Generated-by: LLM
